{
  "$comment": [
    "The 38-letter Urdu qaida inventory.",
    "",
    "joins:  which sides a letter connects on. Getting this wrong makes the glyph",
    "        baker emit positional forms that do not exist in real Urdu.",
    "          both  - connects left and right, so four forms.",
    "          right - connects only to the letter before it, so TWO forms",
    "                  (isolated, final): alif dal Dal zal re Re ze zhe wao",
    "                  bari-ye.",
    "          none  - never connects at all, so ONE form: hamza.",
    "",
    "name:   what the letter is CALLED (bay). Different from sound.",
    "sound:  the phoneme it MAKES (b). Both are taught and both get audio.",
    "",
    "shapeFamily: letters sharing a skeleton and differing only in dots. This is",
    "        the axis Urdu learners actually struggle on, and it drives both the",
    "        shape-families ordering and distractor choice in listen-and-tap.",
    "",
    "word:   foreign key into words.json, or null where no age-appropriate",
    "        picturable word exists. Rare/abstract letters honestly get null",
    "        rather than a word a three-year-old cannot picture."
  ],
